# gate-r.script - level1.script, with a turn assertion before every gameplay
# dump.
#
# WHY THIS SCRIPT EXISTS
#
# Gate R compares frame dumps from two runs byte for byte, which only means
# anything if both dumps were taken at the same point of the simulation.
# level1.script's gameplay dumps fire off `wait`, a number of milliseconds.
#
# WHAT `await turn>N` DOES AND DOES NOT FIX
#
# Amendment 13 added the `turn` metric so a script could wait for the game to
# have run rather than for the clock to have passed. Measured here, it does not
# move the dump instant: by the time the first gameplay dump is due, the flyby
# is over and the simulation is already past turn 300, so all four awaits below
# are satisfied on their first evaluation.
#
#   [smoke] turn held above 0 for 0ms after 0.0s
#   [smoke] turn held above 49 for 0ms after 0.0s
#   [smoke] turn held above 149 for 0ms after 0.0s
#   [smoke] turn held above 299 for 0ms after 0.0s
#
# So they are kept as ASSERTIONS, not as pins: each one fails the run if the
# simulation has not reached that turn by the time the dump is taken, which is
# a real claim about the run and one level1.script does not make. What they are
# not is a way to make a gameplay frame reproducible. See task-19-report.md for
# what is, and is not, comparable on this host.
#
# THE PINNED TURN TRACE, FOR WHOEVER SETS THE THRESHOLDS
#
# Under POP_RECOMP_PIN_CLOCK=1, which is what Gate R runs this script with, the
# turn the simulation has reached at each of the four dump points below is,
# measured from a run that loaded the level:
#
#   dump turn001   turn 609
#   dump turn050   turn 641
#   dump turn150   turn 793
#   dump turn300   turn 878
#
# Every threshold in this file is below all of those, which is why each await
# reports `after 0.0s`. A threshold that makes the wait real has to come from
# above that trace, something like 620, 700, 800 and 900. They are left as they
# are until the `dumpat turn>=N` verb lands: that verb dumps on the first
# present after a turn and is the right mechanism, where a number chosen by
# hand from this trace would be a second thing to keep in step with the script.
#
# WHAT THE PINNED CLOCK DOES AND DOES NOT FIX
#
# It fixes startup completely. Two pinned runs of this script report the same
# line to six decimal places and the same frame count, and their
# smoke_menu_present.ppm is byte-identical:
#
#   [smoke] picture held above 0.98 (reached 0.997708) for 40 frames (2000 ms asked) after 18.9s
#
# It does not fix the clicks. Those two runs diverged at the first click after
# that identical menu: one loaded the level and reached turn 878, the next
# reached turn 0 with zero textures and zero draws. The runner holds a click for
# kClickHoldMs = 120 (smoke_main.mm:607), which under the pin is 2.4 presented
# frames, and level1.script's own header records that a click which does not
# stay down long enough is lost entirely. `guestclick x y left hold <ms>` exists
# for this and parses, but HOST_SCRIPT_GUESTCLICK is not executed by the smoke
# host yet, so there is nothing this script can set. Until it is, Gate R will
# sometimes report that one of its mods-off runs failed, which is the gate
# refusing to compare a level screen against a blank one.
#
# EVERYTHING ELSE IS level1.script, VERBATIM
#
# Every wait, every move, every click. That is deliberate and it was learned
# twice. A version that replaced the two waits after the opening dialog with
# the turn await never started the level at all (`turn did NOT hold above 0
# after 90.0s`, scene brightness 0.204, watchdog at step 18 of 29). A version
# that kept those but dropped the 1.5 s, 10 s and 6 s waits between the
# gameplay dumps ran the level correctly and then failed three of its own
# expectations, because the watched entity never got time to move:
# watch_samples 17 of 100, watch_moved 0 of 256, watch_state_changes 0 of 1.
# The waits are the script; only the assertions are added.
#
# See level1.script for where every coordinate comes from, why moves are
# relative, and why a click has to be held.

await picture>0.98 for 2000 within 60000
dump menu
moveby -2000 -2000
wait 400
moveby 320 140
wait 700
click left 320 140
wait 3000
moveby -2000 -2000
wait 400
moveby 320 219
wait 700
click left 320 219
wait 12000
dump levelselect
moveby -2000 -2000
wait 300
moveby 605 448
wait 900
click left 605 448
wait 8000
dump level_start
moveby -2000 -2000
wait 300
moveby 236 148
wait 700
click left 236 148
wait 4000
dump level_playing
wait 34000
await turn>0 within 60000
dump turn001
peek 0x8e0428 179
watch 0 1
move 331 208
wait 800
click left 331 208
wait 1500
await turn>49 within 60000
dump turn050
move 350 245
wait 800
click left 350 245
wait 10000
await turn>149 within 60000
dump turn150
wait 6000
await turn>299 within 60000
dump turn300
expect textures>0
expect draws>0
expect writebacks>0
expect scene_nonblack>0.50
expect present_nonblack>0.50
expect audio_peak>0.05
expect present_changes>200
expect presents>50
expect entities>0
expect watch_found>0
expect watch_selected>0
expect watch_samples>100
expect watch_moved>256
expect watch_toward_target>0.95
expect watch_state_changes>0
quit
